1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,301
On a Tuesday in October 2018, Patisserie Valerie's owner

2
00:00:04,301 --> 00:00:08,303
was worth about four hundred and fifty million pounds.

3
00:00:08,303 --> 00:00:11,787
By Wednesday afternoon, its shares were frozen.

4
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:14,978
The company said it had been notified

5
00:00:14,978 --> 00:00:19,762
of significant, and potentially fraudulent, accounting irregularities.

6
00:00:20,416 --> 00:00:22,879
Within days, its chairman said he hadn't

7
00:00:22,879 --> 00:00:25,526
even known the company had bank overdrafts.

8
00:00:25,526 --> 00:00:28,973
There were two of them, worth almost ten million pounds.

9
00:00:29,649 --> 00:00:33,956
Eight years on, nobody has been convicted of anything.

10
00:00:33,956 --> 00:00:38,183
The criminal trial has just been pushed back to 2028.

11
00:00:38,783 --> 00:00:42,248
So what does the official record actually establish?

12
00:00:42,248 --> 00:00:46,379
And who saw the warning signs, years earlier, and missed them?

13
00:00:46,379 --> 00:00:48,511
Hold on to that second question.

14
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:54,442
We go through the paperwork: the company's own stock-market announcements, the

15
00:00:54,442 --> 00:00:59,012
audit regulator's sanctions, and the Serious Fraud Office's case file.

16
00:00:59,683 --> 00:01:01,023
Chapter 1.

17
00:01:01,023 --> 00:01:03,971
A cake shop from 1926.

18
00:01:04,616 --> 00:01:08,953
The story starts in 1926, on Frith Street in Soho,

19
00:01:08,953 --> 00:01:13,724
with a Belgian woman remembered only as Madame Valerie.

20
00:01:14,416 --> 00:01:18,485
The first café was bombed during the Second World War.

21
00:01:18,485 --> 00:01:21,950
In the 1940s it moved around the corner to Old

22
00:01:21,950 --> 00:01:26,245
Compton Street, and stayed there for about seventy years.

23
00:01:26,916 --> 00:01:29,974
By 2018 the brand had grown into a listed

24
00:01:29,974 --> 00:01:35,567
company, Patisserie Holdings, with almost two hundred high-street bakeries.

25
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:41,195
On paper it was a rare thing: a cake chain the stock market adored.

26
00:01:41,195 --> 00:01:44,591
On the ninth of October 2018 its shares closed

27
00:01:44,591 --> 00:01:48,208
at four hundred and twenty-nine and a half pence.

28
00:01:48,883 --> 00:01:50,925
The man in the chair was Luke

29
00:01:50,925 --> 00:01:54,868
Johnson, the executive chairman and largest shareholder.

30
00:01:54,868 --> 00:01:57,474
The finance director was Chris Marsh.

31
00:01:58,149 --> 00:02:00,061
Keep that word cash in mind.

32
00:02:00,061 --> 00:02:04,976
Everything in this story turns on how much of it the company really had.

33
00:02:05,583 --> 00:02:06,794
Chapter 2.

34
00:02:06,794 --> 00:02:10,063
Wednesday, 10 October 2018.

35
00:02:10,716 --> 00:02:14,478
On Wednesday the tenth of October 2018, Patisserie

36
00:02:14,478 --> 00:02:17,864
Holdings told the market it had been notified

37
00:02:17,864 --> 00:02:23,131
of significant, and potentially fraudulent, accounting irregularities.

38
00:02:23,883 --> 00:02:29,257
It said the irregularities had significantly impacted its cash position, and

39
00:02:29,257 --> 00:02:33,924
might lead to a material change in its overall financial position.

40
00:02:34,583 --> 00:02:37,828
It asked for its shares to be suspended, and said

41
00:02:37,828 --> 00:02:41,471
its finance chief, Chris Marsh, had been suspended too.

42
00:02:42,149 --> 00:02:44,849
Its shares traded on AIM, the London

43
00:02:44,849 --> 00:02:48,899
Stock Exchange's market for smaller growing companies.

44
00:02:48,899 --> 00:02:52,424
A suspension means nobody can buy or sell them.

45
00:02:52,424 --> 00:02:54,299
Investors were locked in.

46
00:02:54,983 --> 00:02:57,613
There was a second disclosure that day.

47
00:02:57,613 --> 00:03:01,593
The taxman had filed a winding-up petition against the main

48
00:03:01,593 --> 00:03:06,179
trading company, Stonebeach, over one point one four million pounds.

49
00:03:06,849 --> 00:03:11,319
A company valued at four hundred and fifty million pounds, facing

50
00:03:11,319 --> 00:03:15,308
a court petition over one point one million pounds of tax.

51
00:03:15,308 --> 00:03:19,090
That doesn't add up, and it was the first public crack.

52
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:23,291
Luke Johnson said: we are all deeply concerned about

53
00:03:23,291 --> 00:03:26,764
this news and the potential impact on the business.

54
00:03:27,416 --> 00:03:32,940
Two days later, the Serious Fraud Office opened a criminal investigation.

55
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:35,437
Its codename was Operation Venom.

56
00:03:36,049 --> 00:03:39,424
By the following week, the trade press reported that

57
00:03:39,424 --> 00:03:42,668
the finance director had been arrested and bailed.

58
00:03:42,668 --> 00:03:45,329
Being arrested is not a finding of guilt.

59
00:03:45,983 --> 00:03:46,777
Chapter 3.

60
00:03:46,777 --> 00:03:49,396
The overdrafts nobody knew about.

61
00:03:50,049 --> 00:03:54,405
On the twelfth of October the company published a rescue plan.

62
00:03:54,405 --> 00:03:57,004
It began with a remarkable admission.

63
00:03:57,616 --> 00:04:00,565
Its words: historical statements on the cash

64
00:04:00,565 --> 00:04:03,245
position of the Company were mis-stated.

65
00:04:03,916 --> 00:04:07,546
Instead of cash in the bank, the board now estimated

66
00:04:07,546 --> 00:04:11,036
net debt of about nine point eight million pounds.

67
00:04:11,716 --> 00:04:14,664
It said it needed an immediate cash injection

68
00:04:14,664 --> 00:04:17,154
of no less than twenty million pounds.

69
00:04:17,154 --> 00:04:19,643
Without it, there was no scope for the

70
00:04:19,643 --> 00:04:22,657
group to continue trading in its current form.

71
00:04:23,383 --> 00:04:25,469
The money came from two places.

72
00:04:25,469 --> 00:04:30,582
Luke Johnson offered a ten million pound loan for three years, interest free

73
00:04:30,582 --> 00:04:34,686
and fee free, plus a bridging loan of up to ten million more.

74
00:04:35,349 --> 00:04:38,000
And new shares were to be placed at fifty

75
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,232
pence each, to raise about fifteen million pounds.

76
00:04:41,232 --> 00:04:45,952
Three days earlier, the shares had been worth more than eight times that.

77
00:04:46,616 --> 00:04:51,166
Put simply, anyone who bought at the old price had lost almost nine

78
00:04:51,166 --> 00:04:55,444
tenths of it, on paper, before the full picture was even known.

79
00:04:56,116 --> 00:04:58,067
Then the story got stranger.

80
00:04:58,067 --> 00:05:01,900
The company found two overdrafts, at HSBC and Barclays,

81
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:05,454
that had been set up without the board's knowledge.

82
00:05:06,116 --> 00:05:10,127
Nine point seven million pounds had been drawn on them.

83
00:05:10,716 --> 00:05:13,283
Johnson told The Sunday Times: I didn't

84
00:05:13,283 --> 00:05:16,376
even know there were bank overdraft facilities.

85
00:05:16,376 --> 00:05:18,416
He called the week a nightmare.

86
00:05:19,083 --> 00:05:21,675
He added that the banks were as shocked as

87
00:05:21,675 --> 00:05:24,267
the board, and, he was sure, the auditors.

88
00:05:24,267 --> 00:05:25,625
Remember the auditors.

89
00:05:25,625 --> 00:05:27,106
We'll come back to them.

90
00:05:27,783 --> 00:05:28,604
Chapter 4.

91
00:05:28,604 --> 00:05:30,492
The end of the counter.

92
00:05:31,149 --> 00:05:33,729
The rescue bought three months.

93
00:05:33,729 --> 00:05:37,308
In January 2019 the company could not renew

94
00:05:37,308 --> 00:05:41,136
its banking facilities with HSBC and Barclays.

95
00:05:41,816 --> 00:05:46,549
It entered administration, with KPMG appointed as administrator.

96
00:05:46,549 --> 00:05:50,691
The company blamed what it called the significant fraud.

97
00:05:51,283 --> 00:05:55,831
The first wave of closures took in twenty-seven Patisserie Valerie

98
00:05:55,831 --> 00:06:01,689
shops, nineteen Druckers cafés, twenty-five concessions, and the Spitalfields bakery.

99
00:06:02,349 --> 00:06:06,019
Nine hundred and twenty people were made redundant.

100
00:06:06,616 --> 00:06:11,767
On the twenty-third of January 2019, the shop on Old Compton Street,

101
00:06:11,767 --> 00:06:16,311
home to the brand for about seventy years, closed its doors.

102
00:06:16,983 --> 00:06:20,758
Johnson made a further interest-free loan, so staff in

103
00:06:20,758 --> 00:06:24,742
the surviving business could be paid their January wages.

104
00:06:25,416 --> 00:06:28,445
One hundred and twenty-two outlets kept trading

105
00:06:28,445 --> 00:06:31,281
while the administrators looked for a buyer.

106
00:06:31,281 --> 00:06:35,084
Mike Ashley's Sports Direct bid, then withdrew, complaining

107
00:06:35,084 --> 00:06:37,533
it had been locked out of the process.

108
00:06:38,249 --> 00:06:43,266
In February, the Dublin-based investor Causeway Capital Partners bought

109
00:06:43,266 --> 00:06:48,142
ninety-six Patisserie Valerie sites, saving nearly two thousand jobs.

110
00:06:48,816 --> 00:06:50,060
The brand survived.

111
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:53,595
The listed company, and its shareholders' four hundred

112
00:06:53,595 --> 00:06:56,409
and fifty million pound valuation, did not.

113
00:06:57,083 --> 00:06:57,814
Chapter 5.

114
00:06:57,814 --> 00:06:59,643
Where were the auditors?.

115
00:07:00,283 --> 00:07:03,052
Now, that second question.

116
00:07:03,052 --> 00:07:09,336
The company's auditor since 2007 was Grant Thornton, and it

117
00:07:09,336 --> 00:07:14,875
signed clean audit opinions for 2015, 2016 and 2017.

118
00:07:15,583 --> 00:07:18,875
In September 2021 the audit regulator, the

119
00:07:18,875 --> 00:07:23,030
Financial Reporting Council, published its sanctions.

120
00:07:23,030 --> 00:07:28,987
Grant Thornton and its audit partner, David Newstead, had admitted failures.

121
00:07:29,649 --> 00:07:33,096
The admitted failures covered four areas: revenue,

122
00:07:33,096 --> 00:07:36,266
cash, journals, and additions to fixed assets.

123
00:07:36,266 --> 00:07:39,437
They happened in every one of the three years.

124
00:07:40,116 --> 00:07:43,796
The FRC said they reflected lapses in professional

125
00:07:43,796 --> 00:07:48,875
judgement, professional scepticism, audit evidence and documentation.

126
00:07:49,549 --> 00:07:55,215
The regulator's deputy executive counsel, Claudia Mortimore, said the case showed

127
00:07:55,215 --> 00:07:59,272
a serious lack of competence in conducting the audit work.

128
00:07:59,949 --> 00:08:04,812
She listed missed red flags, a failure to obtain enough audit evidence,

129
00:08:04,812 --> 00:08:09,332
and a failure to stand back and question what management provided.

130
00:08:10,016 --> 00:08:13,129
Press reports of the decision gave examples.

131
00:08:13,129 --> 00:08:16,667
Third-party invoices were accepted despite missing

132
00:08:16,667 --> 00:08:19,780
branding, typing errors and wrong addresses.

133
00:08:20,449 --> 00:08:24,405
And in 2016, three quarters of the year's voucher

134
00:08:24,405 --> 00:08:28,521
income was reportedly paid in a single transaction.

135
00:08:28,521 --> 00:08:31,185
The auditors didn't ask about it.

136
00:08:31,849 --> 00:08:32,548
The fines.

137
00:08:32,548 --> 00:08:37,438
Grant Thornton's starting sanction was four million pounds, cut to two

138
00:08:37,438 --> 00:08:42,468
point three four million after discounts for admissions and cooperation.

139
00:08:43,149 --> 00:08:46,717
David Newstead's fine fell from one hundred and fifty

140
00:08:46,717 --> 00:08:51,160
thousand pounds to eighty-seven thousand, seven hundred and fifty,

141
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:53,852
with a three-year ban on signing audits.

142
00:08:54,583 --> 00:08:58,473
Both received a severe reprimand, and a declaration that

143
00:08:58,473 --> 00:09:02,364
each audit report failed to meet the required standards.

144
00:09:03,049 --> 00:09:07,337
The FRC also ordered Grant Thornton to report to it every year

145
00:09:07,337 --> 00:09:11,902
for three years on its fixes, review its culture of challenge, and

146
00:09:11,902 --> 00:09:15,775
accept extra monitoring of its bank and cash audit work.

147
00:09:16,516 --> 00:09:18,521
There was a civil case too.

148
00:09:18,521 --> 00:09:24,459
The administrators sought around two hundred million pounds from Grant Thornton.

149
00:09:24,459 --> 00:09:29,359
In 2022 it was settled for an undisclosed multi-million pound sum.

150
00:09:29,949 --> 00:09:32,542
Luke Johnson, who is not accused of

151
00:09:32,542 --> 00:09:36,097
any wrongdoing, was among the largest creditors.

152
00:09:36,097 --> 00:09:40,022
He has said other executive directors misled him with

153
00:09:40,022 --> 00:09:44,244
a false picture of the finances, Consultancy.uk reported.

154
00:09:44,983 --> 00:09:45,867
Chapter 6.

155
00:09:45,867 --> 00:09:47,457
The criminal case.

156
00:09:48,116 --> 00:09:50,683
Back to the Serious Fraud Office.

157
00:09:50,683 --> 00:09:54,415
In June 2019, five more people were arrested and

158
00:09:54,415 --> 00:09:59,004
interviewed, in a joint operation with three police forces.

159
00:09:59,683 --> 00:10:03,727
Then, nearly five years after the collapse, in

160
00:10:03,727 --> 00:10:07,595
September 2023, the SFO charged four people.

161
00:10:08,283 --> 00:10:12,685
They are Christopher Marsh, the former finance director; Louise

162
00:10:12,685 --> 00:10:16,459
Marsh, an accountant and his wife; Pritesh Mistry, the

163
00:10:16,459 --> 00:10:21,071
financial controller; and Nileshkumar Lad, a financial consultant.

164
00:10:21,816 --> 00:10:26,315
The SFO's director at the time, Lisa Osofsky, said the collapse

165
00:10:26,315 --> 00:10:31,814
rocked our high streets, leaving boarded-up shops and devastating job losses.

166
00:10:32,483 --> 00:10:37,058
The SFO alleges they conspired to inflate the cash in

167
00:10:37,058 --> 00:10:42,237
the company's balance sheets and annual reports from 2015 to

168
00:10:42,237 --> 00:10:47,244
2018, including by giving false documents to the auditors.

169
00:10:48,016 --> 00:10:53,322
The SFO says the company reported twenty-eight million pounds in its accounts,

170
00:10:53,322 --> 00:10:58,288
while ten million pounds of debt was hidden from investors and creditors.

171
00:10:58,288 --> 00:11:02,369
That is the prosecution's case, and it is untested in court.

172
00:11:03,049 --> 00:11:07,659
On the twenty-sixth of April 2024, at Southwark Crown

173
00:11:07,659 --> 00:11:12,008
Court, all four pleaded not guilty to all charges.

174
00:11:12,683 --> 00:11:16,452
The trial was listed for March 2026.

175
00:11:16,452 --> 00:11:20,536
Then, on the ninth of February 2026, it

176
00:11:20,536 --> 00:11:25,143
was relisted for the fourth of January 2028.

177
00:11:25,849 --> 00:11:29,376
The published listings give no reason for the delay.

178
00:11:29,376 --> 00:11:34,191
By then, it will be more than nine years since the investigation began.

179
00:11:34,783 --> 00:11:37,911
So, to be precise: the irregularities and the

180
00:11:37,911 --> 00:11:41,595
hidden overdrafts were the company's own disclosures.

181
00:11:41,595 --> 00:11:44,932
The audit failings are the regulator's findings.

182
00:11:44,932 --> 00:11:48,964
The fraud charges are allegations, and all four deny them.

183
00:11:49,649 --> 00:11:50,688
Chapter 7.

184
00:11:50,688 --> 00:11:52,039
Our analysis.

185
00:11:52,683 --> 00:11:55,244
Here's what we think the paperwork shows.

186
00:11:55,244 --> 00:11:58,805
First: cash is supposed to be the hardest number to fake.

187
00:11:59,416 --> 00:12:01,232
Profits involve judgement.

188
00:12:01,232 --> 00:12:05,213
A bank balance should be one phone call to the bank away.

189
00:12:05,213 --> 00:12:10,381
Yet the company itself admitted its historical cash statements were wrong.

190
00:12:10,983 --> 00:12:14,580
Second: the warning signs weren't hidden in some vault.

191
00:12:14,580 --> 00:12:17,785
The regulator found missed red flags across three

192
00:12:17,785 --> 00:12:20,859
separate audit years, and the firm admitted it.

193
00:12:21,516 --> 00:12:25,102
Third: the first to pay were staff and shareholders.

194
00:12:25,102 --> 00:12:30,206
Nine hundred and twenty staff were made redundant as administration began.

195
00:12:30,206 --> 00:12:35,447
Shareholders saw a four hundred and fifty million pound valuation wiped out.

196
00:12:36,049 --> 00:12:38,260
Fourth: justice is slow.

197
00:12:38,260 --> 00:12:43,785
Irregularities disclosed in 2018 will not reach a jury until

198
00:12:43,785 --> 00:12:49,035
2028, and until then, four people remain innocent in law.

199
00:12:49,716 --> 00:12:50,996
And the comeback?

200
00:12:50,996 --> 00:12:56,719
By September 2023, Time Out reported the chain was down to twenty-nine cafés

201
00:12:56,719 --> 00:13:02,292
from a peak of one hundred and ninety-three, and was reopening some sites.

202
00:13:02,983 --> 00:13:05,191
The new owner inherited a mess.

203
00:13:05,191 --> 00:13:09,038
Time Out reported broken ovens left unused for months,

204
00:13:09,038 --> 00:13:12,743
unpaid suppliers, and a leaking roof at head office.

205
00:13:12,743 --> 00:13:15,307
At one point, money was so short the

206
00:13:15,307 --> 00:13:18,584
chain stopped using butter in its puff pastry.

207
00:13:19,316 --> 00:13:24,175
Its cakes are still sold in around four hundred Sainsbury's supermarkets.

208
00:13:24,175 --> 00:13:27,103
The name outlived the company that owned it.

209
00:13:27,716 --> 00:13:30,719
Patisserie Valerie wasn't killed by bad cakes.

210
00:13:30,719 --> 00:13:33,722
According to the company's own disclosures, it

211
00:13:33,722 --> 00:13:36,334
was killed by numbers that weren't true.

212
00:13:36,983 --> 00:13:40,833
Every source for this episode is numbered in the description.

213
00:13:40,833 --> 00:13:44,620
If we've got something wrong, tell us, and we'll correct it.

214
00:13:45,216 --> 00:13:49,519
The next case file is Wirecard, the German payments company.

215
00:13:49,519 --> 00:13:51,957
Subscribe so it's waiting for you.
